WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.916
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.840
PRESENTER: Our next
speakers will, in fact,

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.650
talk about a specific
relationship between mene

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.860
et manus, between
art and engineering,

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.900
in the world of architecture.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.730
We are very honored
today to have

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:31.860
with us architect IM
Pei, class of 1940,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.130
formerly with the firm of
Pei Cobb Freed &amp; Partners.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:39.830
Of course, Mr. Pei
is very well-known

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.870
for many, many buildings.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:45.320
And I will not list them all.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.260
Some of them are particularly
important for the arts,

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.130
including, of course,
the Louvre, the East

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.880
Wing of the National
Gallery at Washington DC,

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.690
and many MIT alums are familiar
particularly with the Meyerson

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.420
Hall for the Dallas
Symphony Orchestra,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.300
a remarkable building.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:10.190
At MIT, Mr. Pei has not only
designed the Wiesner Building

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:14.810
on Ames Street but the arch
to that building, which

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.640
is a gateway into East Campus
as you cross Ames Street, also--

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.430
not, I think coincidentally--

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.190
when you are walking
toward the campus,

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:30.890
frame his three buildings
in the main quadrangle

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.980
with, in perspective,
the MIT dome

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:37.940
overall, the three buildings on
the main campus with their very

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:42.320
stark geometry of verticals
and horizontals, triangles

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.660
and rectangles.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.950
Most recently-- or not most
recently, but among Mr.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:50.930
Pei's most recent works--

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.270
are the Bank of
China in Hong Kong

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.330
and the Four Seasons
Hotel in New York City.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:59.330
And I think we will be hearing
about some of these works

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.530
today.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:05.240
Mr. Pei has also been
affiliated with MIT since 1972

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.870
as a member of the Council
for the Arts at MIT.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:14.210
And we appreciate his years
of membership on that group.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:20.180
Talking to Mr. Pei today will
be Dean William Mitchell.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:24.770
Dean Mitchell has been
at MIT since 1992,

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.320
and he is particularly
well-known for his book,

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.010
published by The MIT Press,
The Reconfigured Eye, which

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.520
deals with digital
photography and, in fact,

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:39.480
visual images that do not
represent the real world.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.690
[LAUGHING]

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.880
Dean Mitchell is
working very hard

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.090
in the School of Architecture to
lead the School of Architecture

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.110
into the 21st century
with his Virtual Design

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.750
Studios and The Design
Studios of the Future

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:59.040
using computer technology
to create a seamless process

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.430
from the initial designs
through to the communications

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.110
with clients,
engineers, and others.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:09.555
Please help me to welcome IM
Pei and Dean William Mitchell.

00:03:09.555 --> 00:03:12.525
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.060
MITCHELL: Redoing the Louvre--

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.210
that sounds much better.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:53.270
Redoing the Louvre
is an extraordinarily

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:57.800
large and complex project
involving technical problems,

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.790
organizational problems,
political problems, certainly,

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.890
problems of very complex
cultural symbolism.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.400
So what we thought
we'd do today is

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:12.380
to ask Mr. Pei to describe
a little bit of his thought

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.310
processes, what the problem
was about, the solution that he

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:19.160
finally derived.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:23.090
And then, we'll take some
time to discuss this truly

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.200
magnificent project.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.080
So Mr. Pei.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:33.180
PEI: Professor Morrison talked
about hundreds of centuries.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.380
I'm talking today on the
subject of the Louvre

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:41.460
about eight centuries,
the history of 800 years.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:46.188
But these are very important 800
years for the French, at least.

00:04:46.188 --> 00:04:49.176
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:56.330
It's the building of the period
during which French nation was

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:58.960
born.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.650
It's a period that,
I would say, that's

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:09.730
roughly parallel the
recent French history.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:15.740
In fact, it's also a wonderful
symbol for them, at least,

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:18.280
of French civilization.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:23.680
So therefore, the problem of
the Louvre for the architect

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:29.400
is not just a technical
problem, architectural problem.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:36.000
But it's a problem that
has many, many challenges.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.670
I would like to start
off by telling you

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.242
something about the
history of the Louvre.

00:05:41.242 --> 00:05:42.950
Now, I don't have too
much time, but I'll

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:46.980
try to make it very brief
and, therefore, incomplete.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:53.130
It started in 1202 by, I think
that you can call him a French

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:58.500
King, Philippe Auguste,
who built this fortress

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.460
on the right side of the sand
to protect the Ile-de-France,

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:07.350
which is now the Il de la
Cité, which is the place where

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:15.690
the heart of French life at
that time in Paris took place.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:19.830
It was built as a fortress,
le donjon, as they call it.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.290
And it's important
because I wish

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:26.380
I'd taken some slides to show
you what it is like today.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:30.270
But anyway, it was
a wonderful building

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:36.380
where they put prisoners,
where they put ammunition.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:38.972
It's a very important
place to protect,

00:06:38.972 --> 00:06:40.680
let's say, something
that they considered

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:42.570
to be very important.

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:46.720
It lasted as a donjon,
or as a fortress,

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.530
for roughly, I would
say, maybe 200 years.

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:55.285
It did not become really
a place for the Kings

00:06:55.285 --> 00:07:00.280
to live in until about
the end of 14th century.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:05.040
And I think Charles V was the
king that one should remember.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.800
Because that's the
beginning of Louvre,

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.740
not only as a place for the
king to live in but also

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.410
the beginning of French art.

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:16.980
The first library
of France was there,

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:21.150
and the collection of many,
many objects of Charles V

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.000
was displayed there.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.900
Now, for how many
years since then--

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.850
1400 to today?

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.910
I would say 500
years, 600 years.

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:39.410
Nearly all the French kings that
you know of of any importance

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:45.230
have either lived there,
died there, married there,

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:48.490
and born there.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:51.330
So therefore, it's
an important place.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.150
So therefore, this is
not really surprising

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:56.330
that when someone like myself--

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:05.660
I guess I can consider myself
an American of Chinese descent--

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:08.820
has to tamper with a
very important part

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:10.990
of French history.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:14.220
So I'm going to go
through this in two phases

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.170
to show you what I went through.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:23.490
Phase one lasted five
years, from 1983--

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:27.250
six years-- to 1989.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:30.330
And during those
six years, the plan

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:33.750
was laid, how to deal
with the problem.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:34.980
It's a very complex problem.

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:39.709
And the pyramid was built.

00:08:39.709 --> 00:08:44.470
But before the pyramid was
built, we had a lot of trouble.

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:47.830
We wasted two years.

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:51.490
It's a media that I had
to deal with at that time.

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:53.440
And I was totally unprepared.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:57.095
And my French language is just
not adequate for that purpose.

00:08:57.095 --> 00:08:58.720
But I had to deal
with the media to try

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:04.490
to convince the French that
this was the right thing to do.

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:07.540
So therefore, even though
it took six years, but only

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:09.490
four of those six
years were devoted

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:15.050
to architecture and the
building of the phase one.

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:18.290
Now, phase two is
less spectacular

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:21.610
in the sense of
public information

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:25.130
but is perhaps the more
important of the two.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:30.460
Because it completed a
wing called Richelieu.

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:34.100
And without that wing, there
would be no Louvre today.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:39.220
And we couldn't get
that wing until 1985.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:41.500
The wing-- I tell
you what the wing is.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:45.220
The wing was occupied by
the Ministry of Finance ever

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:49.270
since, oh, I would
say Napoleon III.

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:52.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:55.570
Only 200 years-- 150 years.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:57.820
No, less-- 1856--

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:59.340
150 years, yes.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:04.597
And they refused to move.

00:10:04.597 --> 00:10:07.531
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.500
And at that time, the
pyramid is already discussed.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.840
And people say, OK,
if you must have it.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:15.920
Yes, Mitterrand must want it.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:17.050
OK.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.030
But I said, look
here, there's no point

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:23.440
to build the pyramid if we don't
have the Ministry of Finance.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.230
So that gave the president
another headache.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:29.830
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:37.360
But long story short, we
moved them out in 1989.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:41.730
So the second phase is the
building of the Richelieu wing.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:44.070
It's architecturally not,
of course, spectacular

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.060
but very important.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:45.570
Because why?

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:47.460
Because I have to
keep the facade.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:49.950
The only thing I
could do is inside.

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:51.880
And we demolish
everything inside

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:56.190
except a big suite of
Second Empire rooms--

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:59.100
very beautiful rooms,
you must go and see it--

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.860
and two or three staircases.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:05.160
And the rest of Richelieu
was completely gutted.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:08.440
So it was a major
engineering project but not

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:11.460
very spectacular, except there
are some interesting things

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:14.580
inside, which you will
see when you come.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:17.850
So because of time, I'm going
to start with phase one quickly.

00:11:20.067 --> 00:11:21.650
MITCHELL: You have
your slide changer.

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:22.590
PEI: Oh, yes.

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:24.710
I'm going to have to
learn how to use this.

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:25.960
MITCHELL: Press the top there.

00:11:29.046 --> 00:11:29.550
PEI: Nope.

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:29.850
Oh, yeah.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:30.510
MITCHELL: Yes.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:34.080
And this one for the other side.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:35.960
This one here.

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:37.820
PEI: Excuse me.

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:40.580
MITCHELL: We have to sort
out the technology here.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.260
PEI: Oh.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:43.040
Oh, I see.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:44.120
MITCHELL: There we go.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:44.810
PEI: Oh, I see.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:45.650
Now I understand.

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:46.150
OK.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:56.690
Now Louvre, as you
know it, perhaps

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:59.810
without too much debate among
architects and planners,

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:04.200
is perhaps the most important
urban composition in the world

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:04.700
today.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:11.290
There's no other that
can compare with it.

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:13.720
It's history-- 800
years in the building.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:16.150
It started as a
fortress and then was

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:18.880
added on by kings after kings.

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:20.350
But the important
kings to remember

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:22.450
that built the
Louvre are probably,

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:28.750
I would say, Charles V,
Francis I, Henry II, Henry IV,

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:32.680
Louis XIII, Louis
XIV, and eventually,

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:36.110
Napoleon I and Napoleon III.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:38.750
And there are many other kings
in between that added something

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:39.250
to it.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:42.100
But those were the
important kings.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:44.620
And it can also be
said that, perhaps,

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:47.590
every important French
architect-- nearly every,

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:54.925
from Lescot to Mansart to
Le Vau under Louis XIV--

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:02.390
many, many others-- and then
eventually Lefuel, Visconti--

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:05.025
they all participated
in the building of it.

00:13:05.025 --> 00:13:06.400
So therefore, it's
not surprising

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:09.220
that if you come into this
wonderful complex that's

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:13.430
already formed and to try
to do something with it.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:16.180
Now, the reasons why something
had to be done to the Louvre

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:19.420
is for the reason that you
know, that it was built first

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:21.880
as a fortress, and
it was added on

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:25.810
and added on to try to make
it more comfortable for king

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:28.070
after king.

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:30.770
And to turn it into
a museum in 1793,

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:34.355
the convention is a
move that was correct--

00:13:34.355 --> 00:13:35.980
interesting-- because
it became Louvre.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:41.590
It became a public museum for
the first time 200 years ago.

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:44.760
But it was not at all
suitable for a museum

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:50.610
because it was meant for
life, for kings to live in.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:54.090
So the Louvre has never
really worked as a museum.

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:57.500
I was there for the first
time on a fellowship--

00:13:57.500 --> 00:14:00.028
not MIT but Harvard fellowship.

00:14:00.028 --> 00:14:01.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:04.398
I did receive MIT fellowship,
but it was during the war.

00:14:04.398 --> 00:14:05.690
I couldn't do anything with it.

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.000
I live across the
street from the Louvre

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:13.740
in a very tiny, little hotel.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:16.360
And I went over there
every day to look at it.

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:17.780
And I'll tell you,
in those years,

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:20.290
you really have to have time
to see the Louvre because you

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:26.070
get lost in it and you don't
know where anything was.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:29.980
And there are no
toilets, no restaurants--

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:31.730
nothing of that sort.

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:34.360
But it had a
wonderful collection,

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:38.050
and you have to go back time and
time again to find surprises.

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:42.130
And that was the way Louvre was
to all of you, to many others,

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:45.620
until something
happened in 1989.

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:49.100
So therefore, Louvre is a
wonderful complex of buildings.

00:14:49.100 --> 00:14:52.300
But Louvre Museum was not.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:56.560
Louvre Museum happened to
be a tenant in the Louvre.

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:57.700
That's all.

00:14:57.700 --> 00:15:00.880
It was occupied by the
Ministry of Culture

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:02.770
running the Museum of France.

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:07.120
It was occupied by the
Ministry of Finance,

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:09.130
occupied by many, many others.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:14.590
Louvre Museum occupy a long,
long wing along the Seine.

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:18.220
And it's about, I would
say, 800 meters long.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:21.670
And that wing was
almost impossible to go

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:23.560
from one end to the
other without going up

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:25.990
and down and there.

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:28.380
So therefore, most people
who went to the Louvre,

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:34.090
as I did, probably only
saw, maybe, 25% of it.

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:36.540
And the rest, you just miss.

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:38.487
You have to have a guide.

00:15:38.487 --> 00:15:39.570
And I didn't have a guide.

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:41.460
So therefore, I had to
go back time and time

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:44.220
again to find new
things, new surprises.

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:46.860
So Louvre did not
work as a museum.

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:49.280
And the French knew it.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:51.720
But they wanted, finally,
under Mitterrand,

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:53.620
to do something about it.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:56.850
And the one move that was
perhaps the most important move

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:58.560
of all I mentioned earlier.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:02.940
It was the recapturing
of the Richelieu wing

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:04.782
from the Ministry of Finance.

00:16:04.782 --> 00:16:07.554
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:15.600
Now, this is perhaps
not a very good slide.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:16.530
I apologize.

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:19.230
But you can see the
importance of Louvre

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:21.360
in the heart of Paris.

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:24.600
Because it's situated perhaps
in the most important--

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:25.710
really in center--

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:28.050
heart of Paris.

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:31.750
And yet, it separated the
left bank from the right bank.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:35.370
There's no way to
penetrate except by car

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:41.280
from left bank, which is
below to the right bank.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:44.660
So it became,
actually, a barrier.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:48.660
And that became an urban design
problem of first magnitude.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:51.090
Because we have to
open up the Louvre.

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:54.910
To open up the Louvre, then you
rejoin the two parts of Paris,

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:56.190
not by car--

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:59.670
you can always drive
around-- but on foot.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:01.290
And this is one of
the challenges that

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:04.230
is least talked about but
has, in my opinion, the most

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:05.174
important.

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:13.750
The access of the Louvre leads
all the way to Saint-Germain.

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:16.839
And it all, probably,
is, again, when

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:19.420
I say the most important
urban composition,

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:23.260
I really should say that
it's the most important axis

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:24.579
of the world.

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:26.470
There's no axis
like that that leads

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:29.830
all the way from the
Louvre to Saint-Germain.

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:32.900
It went through the Garden of
the Louvre, which you all know,

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:36.830
Place de La Concorde, up
Champs-Élysées to the Arc de

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:40.140
Triomphe and then on to La
Défense and then La Défense

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:48.880
[? beyond. ?] Now, this is a
diagram of the Louvre to show

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:56.390
you the gray area on top
was occupied by the Ministry

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:57.930
of Finance.

00:17:57.930 --> 00:18:01.333
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:01.333 --> 00:18:07.940
The ochre, the yellowish
color wing, 800 meters long--

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:09.470
half a mile long--

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:12.530
occupied not just by the
museum, by the museum

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:14.930
and by the museum
administration of France.

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:18.486
And French bureaucracy, I tell
you, takes up a lot of space.

00:18:18.486 --> 00:18:21.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:25.680
So it's not all museum.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:34.260
You can see that people come
by one side of the courtyard

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:36.300
and enter there
while you're waiting

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:38.670
in line if you're lucky--

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:40.373
a good day.

00:18:40.373 --> 00:18:41.790
You don't mind,
but then you'll be

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:45.960
pestered by people trying to
sell you all kinds of things

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:47.850
that you don't want.

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:50.460
But nonetheless, the people--

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:53.130
three million people--
continue to come.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:53.630
Why?

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:55.380
For this collection.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:57.740
But you really have
to go through, really,

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:01.140
a very uncomfortable
experience in the process.

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:03.540
Now, what I saw
at that time when

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:06.690
I was asked by the
French government to say,

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:09.490
can you do something
about the museum?

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:11.070
I said, I don't know.

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:13.020
But anyway, I'll try.

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:15.550
And the one thing that
became apparent to me,

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:19.230
when I look at this problem,
I said the Ministry of Finance

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:19.999
has to go.

00:19:19.999 --> 00:19:24.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:27.180
And I said, the
reason is simple.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:32.840
I said, when you want to
build a modern museum, Louvre,

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:34.610
why it is not a modern museum--

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:35.660
for simple reason.

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:38.160
And that is in modern museum--

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:41.630
which I knew well then because
I finished the National

00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:43.400
Gallery already--

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:46.430
about 50% of exhibit
space has to be matched

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:51.890
by 50% of supporting spaces--

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:56.390
reserve, conservation
laboratories, restaurants,

00:19:56.390 --> 00:20:02.810
auditorium, lecture halls,
public reception spaces,

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:05.530
toilets, things like
that-- which it did have--

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:07.340
tiny little toilets.

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:10.900
In fact, I remember very
well that when I went there,

00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:12.920
I frequently had to leave
the Louvre because I

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:15.240
have to find a place to go.

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:19.040
And then, when you
leave the Louvre,

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:20.930
some people don't
come back again.

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:23.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:26.780
So they lost a lot of people.

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:33.530
And it's not really surprising
that the average stay

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:39.250
of visitors to the Louvre
is only one hour and a half,

00:20:39.250 --> 00:20:42.410
whereas the National Gallery
is three and a half hours.

00:20:42.410 --> 00:20:45.560
Metropolitan Museum--
about the same.

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:48.770
So I said that the
key to making Louvre

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:53.450
function as a museum is to
change the Louvre from 800

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:56.420
meter long--

00:20:56.420 --> 00:20:58.190
up and down, up and down--

00:20:58.190 --> 00:21:01.430
to something very compact.

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:03.650
But you can give up this
wing, the floor where

00:21:03.650 --> 00:21:05.480
the Spanish paintings were--

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:09.380
or still are-- and
give it to other uses.

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:13.226
But you must move the
Louvre to that part.

00:21:13.226 --> 00:21:15.930
And that way, you
accomplish two things,

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:19.220
which is essential to making
Louvre Museum function.

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:22.190
Number one, you can
excavate that court--

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:24.170
which is in gray area--

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:26.660
the Napoleon court.

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:28.230
We can go down two levels.

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:30.500
It goes to the
level of the Seine,

00:21:30.500 --> 00:21:32.960
which is about 10 meters down.

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:34.550
You can go down two levels.

00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:37.820
And you can recapture half a
million square feet of space

00:21:37.820 --> 00:21:39.710
just by excavating that.

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:40.760
And why not?

00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:42.770
You can use that
space for reserve.

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:45.830
You can use that space
for all the infrastructure

00:21:45.830 --> 00:21:48.620
support that a
modern museum needs.

00:21:48.620 --> 00:21:50.930
Because under the old Louvre,
there's no foundation.

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:52.235
There's no space.

00:21:52.235 --> 00:21:54.800
They have some pipes
and that's about all.

00:21:54.800 --> 00:21:58.970
But nothing there-- there's
some sewers that go through.

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:04.300
But that is the key
to making the Louvre

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:05.990
into the modern museum.

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:10.345
Second reason is that if you
put the center of the Louvre

00:22:10.345 --> 00:22:12.860
not on one side, down
below, but really

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:15.860
in the middle of that
gray area, you've

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:20.410
got the center of gravity
of the Louvre right there.

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:23.330
And from that point, you
can go to the three wings.

00:22:23.330 --> 00:22:24.650
Louvre had three wings.

00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:26.010
Do you know?

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:29.880
Venus de Milo is there,
and Mona Lisa is there.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:35.370
And to Richelieu and to
Sully, very short distance--

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:37.467
not 800 meters anymore.

00:22:37.467 --> 00:22:39.300
It's going to be only
about something that's

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:42.840
50 meters, the difference so
that you can divide Louvre

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:46.470
into, really, three parts, even
though they're interconnected

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:49.290
upstairs-- three
parts, and each part

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:52.740
is at least several days' visit.

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:55.380
So a big museum, you
may wonder whether you

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:56.940
need a museum this size.

00:22:56.940 --> 00:23:00.120
But be that as it may,
it's a big museum,

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:02.440
and that's the only way
to solve the problem.

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:06.205
So therefore, I
told the president,

00:23:06.205 --> 00:23:08.220
I said, you've got
to do two things.

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:13.560
You've got to let us
dig under the court.

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:14.998
Because there may
be relics there.

00:23:14.998 --> 00:23:17.040
The project could have
been stopped if they found

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:19.420
something important there.

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:21.090
And then, you also
have to find a way

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:22.980
to move the Ministry of Finance.

00:23:22.980 --> 00:23:25.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:30.640
At this point, it's not
so funny at that time.

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:33.040
[LAUGHING]

00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:40.340
But this tells you something
about the man who is still

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:43.490
the president of France.

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:44.990
I don't know of
any heads of state

00:23:44.990 --> 00:23:50.660
in the world that has the kind
of breadth of understanding

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:54.980
of the history and
culture not only of France

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:56.350
but of the world.

00:23:56.350 --> 00:23:57.890
He's really a remarkable man.

00:23:57.890 --> 00:24:01.038
He may not be the
greatest politician.

00:24:01.038 --> 00:24:01.580
I don't know.

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:03.200
Time will tell.

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:07.160
But he was and he is a really
exceptional leader when

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:09.480
it comes to art and culture.

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:14.430
And I could not have done
anything there without him.

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:15.100
That's obvious.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:22.890
The court, before we
did anything with it,

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:26.160
was a parking lot.

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:31.950
It's a parking lot for the
bureaucrats that occupied

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:33.000
the Ministry of Finance.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:35.325
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:43.210
The court, after excavation--

00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:47.110
one year, archaeologists were
digging, really, literally,

00:24:47.110 --> 00:24:48.700
with brushes.

00:24:48.700 --> 00:24:50.560
And they found
things, all right,

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:52.750
but nothing terribly important.

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:55.640
Because this court,
unlike the court

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:59.200
which I wish I had slides to
show you with the marvelous

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:02.170
donjon foundation,
is now on display,

00:25:02.170 --> 00:25:03.970
which, when you
go there, you see.

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:08.870
This court was inhabited by
people who served the kings.

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:10.080
They're bakers.

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:11.950
They're cobblers.

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:12.990
There's a little church.

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:14.380
And that's about all.

00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:17.740
And therefore, there's
nothing that is so important

00:25:17.740 --> 00:25:19.570
that they say stop.

00:25:19.570 --> 00:25:21.310
We were very fortunate.

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:24.970
From '84 to '85,
that year, we were

00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:27.160
waiting to see what happened.

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:29.250
We wish they discover nothing.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:31.000
And the archaeologists,
of course,

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:34.310
wish they had found something.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:36.850
But anyway, they found
nothing of importance,

00:25:36.850 --> 00:25:42.070
and we proceeded to
get the green light.

00:25:42.070 --> 00:25:45.990
Now, here I want to show you
the organization of the Louvre.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:47.183
This is the court.

00:25:47.183 --> 00:25:48.850
The rest of the
Louvre's not shown here.

00:25:48.850 --> 00:25:50.820
It's only the Napoleon Court.

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:53.850
By putting an object--

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:57.150
something-- in the
center of this court--

00:25:57.150 --> 00:25:59.250
let's say Napoleon Court--

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:01.650
you can connect
the three pavilions

00:26:01.650 --> 00:26:05.490
in a very short and direct
way and very understandable.

00:26:05.490 --> 00:26:06.150
You can see it.

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:10.350
When you are in the court
or in the hall below,

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:14.250
you can look out, and you
can see Denon, Richelieu,

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:15.450
and Sully.

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:19.890
And that clarity of orientation
is key to a big museum.

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:30.030
This is an urban study.

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:34.450
So to try to ventilate
Paris, you must first

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:36.250
ventilate the Louvre.

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:39.770
To ventilate the Louvre, you
have to do several things.

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:43.810
You can come over from the left
bank over Pont des Arts, which

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:45.220
is a pedestrian bridge--

00:26:45.220 --> 00:26:47.800
a wonderful bridge,
architecturally.

00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:50.530
From engineering point of
view, it's a wonderful bridge.

00:26:50.530 --> 00:26:56.320
And then you enter into Cour
Carrée and then turn left.

00:26:56.320 --> 00:27:00.580
But you have to do two things
in order to make it truly open.

00:27:00.580 --> 00:27:04.880
One is that you have to open
that passage under Richelieu,

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:08.740
called Passage Richelieu,
to lead you to the Place du

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:11.420
Palais-Royale, which is right--

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:13.360
and then, from then
on, to Palais-Royale,

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:17.870
and then to Pompidou
Center, which you know.

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:20.770
The second thing
that you must also do

00:27:20.770 --> 00:27:23.040
is to open up the garden.

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:25.420
A new bridge is
being built. I think

00:27:25.420 --> 00:27:28.900
it's about to start construction
here so that you can walk

00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:33.730
from Orsay, which is where
the wonderful Impressionist

00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:37.630
collection is today, from Orsay
all the way to the Louvre.

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:40.510
And that will open
up the Louvre.

00:27:40.510 --> 00:27:44.020
And once that Louvre is opened
up, it no longer is a barrier.

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:46.314
It becomes a connector.

00:27:50.490 --> 00:27:54.720
This was a drawing done
by Steve, who's here,

00:27:54.720 --> 00:28:01.890
Steve [? Oves. ?] I show this
drawing to the president.

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:04.080
And he was not shocked.

00:28:04.080 --> 00:28:07.200
Perhaps he was the only
man that was not shocked.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:14.090
But when I show it to the
press, they were shocked.

00:28:14.090 --> 00:28:16.680
And I tell you, from
that point on, as I say,

00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:22.260
18 months of just nothing
but harassment by the media.

00:28:26.510 --> 00:28:31.130
But still, we got
the green light.

00:28:31.130 --> 00:28:37.250
And the president, as
well as his official,

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:39.670
who was in charge of the
Louvre-- an important man,

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:40.970
Biasini--

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:42.500
said, let's dig.

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:46.860
Because the archaeologists
has already said it's OK.

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:47.840
Let's dig.

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:50.000
And this was what you see.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:55.280
And that was the beginning
of another furor and people

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:59.600
demonstrating and everything
except they didn't

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:03.320
do anything really serious
like committing suicide

00:29:03.320 --> 00:29:06.230
and like that, but very close--

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:07.070
very close.

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:08.657
There were a few.

00:29:08.657 --> 00:29:11.790
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:11.790 --> 00:29:13.860
Now I'm going to go
quickly because of time.

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:18.620
Quickly, to show you how
the building process did.

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:21.010
And the building of this
palace was remarkably fast.

00:29:21.010 --> 00:29:24.480
The French contractors
are incredibly good.

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:27.440
They're very, very good.

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:30.830
These are slides that
shows the construction.

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:32.715
This is Napoleon
Court, by the way.

00:29:32.715 --> 00:29:33.590
This is not the rest.

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:35.010
It's only one part--

00:29:35.010 --> 00:29:36.110
the important part.

00:29:36.110 --> 00:29:38.780
This is the part where
the pyramid must emerge.

00:29:38.780 --> 00:29:42.470
And the square grid that you
see, the structural steel

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:45.240
member there, they're
all on bearings.

00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:46.287
It can move.

00:29:46.287 --> 00:29:47.660
[CHUCKLING]

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:49.910
It can move.

00:29:49.910 --> 00:29:52.250
And they're supported on
four columns, and that's all.

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:04.160
We had about 60 or some slides
because from one vantage point,

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:07.218
they took pictures
every so many months.

00:30:07.218 --> 00:30:08.510
But I'm only showing you a few.

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:14.510
So that's very quick.

00:30:14.510 --> 00:30:15.840
But it took--

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:17.200
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:18.670
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:19.650
Thank you.

00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:26.100
It took 18 months
to reach this stage.

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:31.390
And at this point, I would
say, it's the end of phase one.

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:36.440
It was open in spring 1989.

00:30:36.440 --> 00:30:41.340
And Mitterrand was
there to cut the ribbon.

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:44.870
And it was rather
well-received by then.

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:48.980
But still I would say
there were, maybe,

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:54.120
30% or 40% people still
not very happy with it

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:57.140
and for any number of reasons.

00:30:57.140 --> 00:31:00.500
But nevertheless, it was built.

00:31:00.500 --> 00:31:10.215
And since this is MIT, I thought
I have to bring this slide.

00:31:10.215 --> 00:31:12.532
If I show it to
another audience,

00:31:12.532 --> 00:31:13.990
they would not be
interested in it.

00:31:16.550 --> 00:31:18.850
And I'm going to show
you some of the slides

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:20.650
of the construction.

00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:22.870
But one thing that
should also interest you

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:26.270
that the French government
make no secret of it,

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:29.020
they want everything
to be made in France.

00:31:29.020 --> 00:31:32.740
And the only exception,
which I asked the president

00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:39.010
to allow us, is to have all
the tension elements made

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:46.330
in Massachusetts by a small
rigging company that did

00:31:46.330 --> 00:31:53.835
the America's Cup, the yachts.

00:31:53.835 --> 00:31:55.710
I don't know whether
Bill Coke is here or not

00:31:55.710 --> 00:31:57.275
but he would know.

00:31:57.275 --> 00:31:57.960
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:57.960 --> 00:32:01.190
And we are the best.

00:32:01.190 --> 00:32:04.480
And I showed him some
samples of the rigging,

00:32:04.480 --> 00:32:08.330
how the 10 buckles fit into
the cable and all that.

00:32:08.330 --> 00:32:10.030
And he was fascinated with it.

00:32:10.030 --> 00:32:12.310
And because he liked it so
much that he didn't even

00:32:12.310 --> 00:32:16.340
bother to say, is there a
French equivalent to it?

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:17.666
We just say, go ahead.

00:32:17.666 --> 00:32:19.445
So we did it.

00:32:19.445 --> 00:32:26.470
And on the other hand,
the glass problem.

00:32:26.470 --> 00:32:30.910
You see, glass has to be
white, has to be clear.

00:32:30.910 --> 00:32:32.560
If the glass is not clear--

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:35.240
this is laminated, you
know, it's double layer.

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:35.920
It's very thick.

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:38.400
It's about almost
3/4 of an inch thick.

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:40.390
If it's not clear,
it will be green.

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:43.360
And you will see it through
the corner of this glass.

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:46.270
It will be very dark
green, bottle green.

00:32:46.270 --> 00:32:49.450
So therefore, that is not
acceptable because the Louvre,

00:32:49.450 --> 00:32:52.210
you see, the composition
of the Louvre must be seen.

00:32:52.210 --> 00:32:55.420
And the ochre color stone
shouldn't look green.

00:32:55.420 --> 00:33:01.240
So therefore, I requested
the French manufacturer,

00:33:01.240 --> 00:33:03.520
Saint-Gobain, to
make this glass.

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:04.030
They say no.

00:33:04.030 --> 00:33:05.643
We no longer make them.

00:33:05.643 --> 00:33:07.060
And then, finally,
they say, well,

00:33:07.060 --> 00:33:10.180
if you built 1000 pyramids,
we'll make them for you.

00:33:13.910 --> 00:33:17.690
So I didn't report
this to the president.

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:20.440
I went to a German
firm, Schott Glass.

00:33:20.440 --> 00:33:21.610
I say, can you make it?

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:22.760
Yes, we can.

00:33:22.760 --> 00:33:24.526
Saint-Gobain said,
we'll make it.

00:33:24.526 --> 00:33:26.956
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:31.820 --> 00:33:33.490
I'm going to go very quickly--

00:33:33.490 --> 00:33:34.870
the making of the pyramid.

00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:36.930
The pyramid has one virtue.

00:33:36.930 --> 00:33:38.310
It's a very stable form.

00:33:38.310 --> 00:33:39.990
You know that.

00:33:39.990 --> 00:33:43.380
And consequently, it requires
the least amount of steel

00:33:43.380 --> 00:33:45.210
in order to support it.

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:51.290
And consequently, if you
use the finest technology

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:54.110
available to you,
technologically, to build it,

00:33:54.110 --> 00:33:57.020
it will be the most
transparent form.

00:33:57.020 --> 00:33:59.480
And it must be the
most transparent form

00:33:59.480 --> 00:34:02.210
because you want to
see that composition.

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:04.520
That composition is so
important to the world,

00:34:04.520 --> 00:34:06.020
not just to France,

00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:18.980
So you may ask, do you need
something that projects?

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:22.010
Many architects in
France, as well as abroad,

00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:27.449
suggested, why not just have
a glass sheet on the ground,

00:34:27.449 --> 00:34:29.775
and you bring light
in just as well?

00:34:29.775 --> 00:34:30.400
But I said, no.

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:33.639
I said, you have to have space.

00:34:33.639 --> 00:34:38.909
You go into the lower level 10
meters down, nine meters down,

00:34:38.909 --> 00:34:41.420
and you don't want to
have a glass ceiling.

00:34:41.420 --> 00:34:44.251
That word may mean
something to some.

00:34:44.251 --> 00:34:46.015
[LAUGHING]

00:34:46.015 --> 00:34:48.170
You don't want a glass ceiling.

00:34:48.170 --> 00:34:49.639
You want space.

00:34:49.639 --> 00:34:52.870
And so something has to project.

00:34:52.870 --> 00:34:55.719
And I defended the pyramid.

00:34:55.719 --> 00:34:59.130
And the pyramid, for those
of you who know France,

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:01.870
is a very important
symbol to French.

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:05.620
I think the French are probably
more conscious of pyramid

00:35:05.620 --> 00:35:11.350
as a form than any other people
because of Napoleon, I guess.

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:13.500
They really went to Egypt.

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:15.250
They took a lot of
things away from Egypt.

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:21.980
So the pyramid form is
necessary to give you space.

00:35:21.980 --> 00:35:26.590
So give you light, give you
space, but at the same time,

00:35:26.590 --> 00:35:28.630
transparent.

00:35:28.630 --> 00:35:30.640
You can see through it.

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:32.890
And also, you need a symbol.

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:35.230
Because if this is going
to be the main entrance

00:35:35.230 --> 00:35:39.047
to the Louvre, it cannot
be just a subway entrance,

00:35:39.047 --> 00:35:39.880
so simply cannot be.

00:35:43.050 --> 00:35:46.780
Glass was put in this manner.

00:35:46.780 --> 00:35:49.540
And for a long time,
there was a debate.

00:35:49.540 --> 00:35:51.910
Can you clean the glass?

00:35:51.910 --> 00:35:54.335
And we tried different ways.

00:35:54.335 --> 00:35:58.660
They hired some Indians
from Canada to clean it.

00:35:58.660 --> 00:36:01.090
They even tried robot.

00:36:01.090 --> 00:36:02.570
Eventually, we clean by robot.

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:06.760
But finally, we
got some alpinist

00:36:06.760 --> 00:36:09.940
to get up there, hang the rope
from the tip of the pyramid,

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:10.920
and wash it.

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:12.680
And it only took
two days to wash it.

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:13.630
But now, it took less.

00:36:13.630 --> 00:36:15.506
Now, one day is all we need.

00:36:15.506 --> 00:36:18.670
So the washing problem
no longer a problem.

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:20.950
But for a while, that
became also a problem.

00:36:20.950 --> 00:36:24.280
Because the French want to find
any reason to object to it.

00:36:24.280 --> 00:36:27.865
And cleaning the pyramid
also kept us quite involved.

00:36:30.580 --> 00:36:32.410
Now, you're inside the space.

00:36:32.410 --> 00:36:36.303
And you can see the Louvre.

00:36:36.303 --> 00:36:37.720
You can see the
Louvre through it.

00:36:45.336 --> 00:36:46.300
Ah.

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:48.010
That was D-day.

00:36:48.010 --> 00:36:50.505
[LAUGHING]

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:55.460
But no booing-- no booing.

00:36:55.460 --> 00:36:57.432
Lots of applause, no booing.

00:37:11.671 --> 00:37:12.190
Ah.

00:37:12.190 --> 00:37:13.250
I like that photograph.

00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:14.208
You don't see anything.

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:25.385
There's a story
about this statue.

00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:30.826
During Louis XIV's time--

00:37:30.826 --> 00:37:33.130
I'm short of time.

00:37:33.130 --> 00:37:35.120
I would try to go quickly.

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:40.260
Louis XIV's time, they invited--

00:37:40.260 --> 00:37:42.850
and this shows that in those
years, the kings of France

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:46.210
are already very
cultured people--

00:37:46.210 --> 00:37:51.240
he invited Bernini
from Rome to come.

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:59.840
Bernini had just finished the
St. Peter's arcade, that wing

00:37:59.840 --> 00:38:02.640
that enclosed that space.

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:04.970
Perhaps [INAUDIBLE],,
he and Borromini

00:38:04.970 --> 00:38:07.530
were the two most important
architects of that time.

00:38:07.530 --> 00:38:09.470
And the French king
wanted the best,

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:12.740
so he invited Bernini
to come to do something

00:38:12.740 --> 00:38:14.780
in the back of the Louvre--
not the this part--

00:38:14.780 --> 00:38:16.880
in the back of the Louvre.

00:38:16.880 --> 00:38:18.290
And he was there six months.

00:38:18.290 --> 00:38:23.000
And he did not survive
the French architects.

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:27.200
And the only thing he left
behind-- there a few things

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:29.840
he left behind,
not of importance.

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:32.510
But the only big thing
that he left behind

00:38:32.510 --> 00:38:34.375
is a statue of Louis XIV.

00:38:34.375 --> 00:38:37.880
But that was made in Rome,
and it was shipped to France

00:38:37.880 --> 00:38:39.860
afterwards.

00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:43.400
But Louis XIV never liked it
because his image at that time

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:44.900
was 25 years ago.

00:38:44.900 --> 00:38:46.040
He was a young man then.

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:47.660
He's no longer young.

00:38:47.660 --> 00:38:51.750
So he banish it to Versailles,
and it remained there.

00:38:51.750 --> 00:38:54.440
And because it was not known,
it was never vandalized

00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:55.510
and never broke--

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:58.580
I mean it was never
destroyed during the war

00:38:58.580 --> 00:39:01.320
because they just left there.

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:02.700
But it was marble.

00:39:02.700 --> 00:39:07.400
It was vandalized by
a man from Brittany--

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:12.330
anarchist, I guess-- and
no longer salvageable.

00:39:12.330 --> 00:39:17.550
But I persuaded the
conservator of Versailles

00:39:17.550 --> 00:39:19.670
to let us make a cast of it--

00:39:19.670 --> 00:39:21.170
the only way.

00:39:21.170 --> 00:39:21.920
And they did.

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:23.480
And it's made in lead.

00:39:23.480 --> 00:39:27.710
And I thought, we should
put it there to remember him

00:39:27.710 --> 00:39:30.804
as someone who tried.

00:39:30.804 --> 00:39:33.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:36.670
But that very important,
that location.

00:39:36.670 --> 00:39:39.840
I needed something there
to terminate the access

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:44.190
of Chance-Élysées because
that axis of Le Notre--

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:45.840
the Le Notre axis--

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:47.190
was not terminated at Louvre.

00:39:47.190 --> 00:39:49.410
Because the Louvre is a
little bit like this--

00:39:49.410 --> 00:39:50.400
like this.

00:39:50.400 --> 00:39:54.080
So it has to be terminated--
not by the pyramid,

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:56.580
by something else, and
something that's strong.

00:39:56.580 --> 00:40:01.240
And I was fearful of
commissioning a French sculptor

00:40:01.240 --> 00:40:01.800
at that time.

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:05.310
You don't know what's
going to happen.

00:40:05.310 --> 00:40:07.290
I'm perfectly frank about it.

00:40:07.290 --> 00:40:08.870
You just don't know what--

00:40:08.870 --> 00:40:13.464
so that was my escape
from responsibility.

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:19.070
Now, the second
phase of the Louvre--

00:40:19.070 --> 00:40:24.260
not spectacular, not
at all polemical,

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:25.790
but extremely important.

00:40:25.790 --> 00:40:29.870
Because now, it opened
only a few months ago.

00:40:29.870 --> 00:40:32.990
If you go there
now, you understand

00:40:32.990 --> 00:40:36.390
why the pyramid was put
there in the first place.

00:40:36.390 --> 00:40:41.420
And I think the vindication
of the whole plan

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:45.950
is now made possible through
the completion of this wing.

00:40:45.950 --> 00:40:48.200
And for that reason, even
though architecturally, it's

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:51.410
not spectacular because
the facade had to be kept

00:40:51.410 --> 00:40:53.300
and everything has
to be internalized,

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:55.760
done inside-- number
two I had to work

00:40:55.760 --> 00:40:58.070
with two French architects,
which is reasonable.

00:40:58.070 --> 00:41:00.740
Because after all,
I can't hog it all.

00:41:10.070 --> 00:41:13.918
Now, you see under
the Napoleon Court,

00:41:13.918 --> 00:41:15.210
and this is what it looks like.

00:41:15.210 --> 00:41:18.260
This is 10 meters below ground.

00:41:18.260 --> 00:41:21.470
You go up three sets of
escalators to a intermediate

00:41:21.470 --> 00:41:23.210
level-- still below ground--

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:27.500
and you can enter into the
three wings of the Louvre.

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:30.700
And the fourth wing,
going this way,

00:41:30.700 --> 00:41:36.500
goes to shops,
parking, bus terminal.

00:41:36.500 --> 00:41:39.663
Eventually, all the buses
that you see on Rue de Rivoli,

00:41:39.663 --> 00:41:41.080
as well as on the
[? Quai, ?] will

00:41:41.080 --> 00:41:43.110
disappear, as they have now.

00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:44.480
They're all underground now.

00:41:44.480 --> 00:41:46.910
So urbanistically, that's
another very important

00:41:46.910 --> 00:41:48.845
contribution.

00:41:48.845 --> 00:41:51.285
And below, at this lower
level, we have auditorium.

00:41:51.285 --> 00:41:52.360
We have restaurants.

00:41:52.360 --> 00:41:55.100
We have a reception area
for the young people.

00:41:55.100 --> 00:41:58.430
And we have a bookstore--
enormous bookstore--

00:41:58.430 --> 00:42:04.040
and the shops and meeting
rooms and conference center.

00:42:04.040 --> 00:42:05.360
Everything is there.

00:42:05.360 --> 00:42:07.770
But below this level,
it's all circulation.

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:11.390
There is at truckway that
connect all the departments

00:42:11.390 --> 00:42:12.350
underground.

00:42:12.350 --> 00:42:15.737
And there's a large reserve
so that all the collection--

00:42:15.737 --> 00:42:18.320
nearly all the collection of the
Louvre-- now comes back home.

00:42:22.810 --> 00:42:25.055
Now, we have to talk briefly
about Richelieu wing,

00:42:25.055 --> 00:42:26.680
even though,
architecturally, as I say,

00:42:26.680 --> 00:42:28.580
it's not very spectacular.

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:35.540
The two courts were proposed
way back to be covered.

00:42:35.540 --> 00:42:40.270
They were parking and trucking
for the Ministry of Finance

00:42:40.270 --> 00:42:41.980
before, so not used.

00:42:41.980 --> 00:42:45.820
By covering it, we can
turn it into an exhibition

00:42:45.820 --> 00:42:48.580
space for French sculpture.

00:42:48.580 --> 00:42:53.710
And further, we also proposed
to dig down below the Richelieu

00:42:53.710 --> 00:42:56.290
wing, as you see, so
that the two courts are

00:42:56.290 --> 00:42:57.550
connected at the lower level.

00:43:02.260 --> 00:43:06.880
And this was a
trucking area, which

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:10.337
now become exhibition
area for French sculpture.

00:43:26.410 --> 00:43:31.510
This wing house
four departments--

00:43:31.510 --> 00:43:36.130
the sculpture department,
the oriental antiquities

00:43:36.130 --> 00:43:40.990
department, the objet
d'art, which is probably

00:43:40.990 --> 00:43:44.440
the most important objet
d'art collection in the world,

00:43:44.440 --> 00:43:46.430
and paintings.

00:43:53.790 --> 00:44:01.170
I put this in mostly,
again, because this is MIT.

00:44:01.170 --> 00:44:07.260
This is a pyramid, about
50 feet square, inverted.

00:44:07.260 --> 00:44:08.280
Why inverted?

00:44:08.280 --> 00:44:11.850
Because its positioned
at the place where

00:44:11.850 --> 00:44:15.960
the circular rotary is.

00:44:15.960 --> 00:44:18.660
And you don't want to see any
projection there coming up.

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:21.960
Because one pyramid
is quite enough.

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:24.180
But to use the same theme--

00:44:24.180 --> 00:44:26.040
and we wanted to
bring light in--

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:27.490
this is the intersection.

00:44:27.490 --> 00:44:30.930
If you go to the bus
terminal, to the parking,

00:44:30.930 --> 00:44:33.340
and to the shops, you have
to go through this point.

00:44:33.340 --> 00:44:35.380
So therefore, it's
nice to have something

00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:38.080
to make people feel they're
still in the Louvre.

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:44.760
So now, this suspended pyramid
is a major engineering project.

00:44:44.760 --> 00:44:48.330
It was designed by
a man, Peter Rice

00:44:48.330 --> 00:44:50.940
of Arup Associates in London.

00:44:50.940 --> 00:44:53.260
And too bad I don't
have the drawings.

00:44:53.260 --> 00:44:55.860
It's actually a very
brilliant design.

00:44:55.860 --> 00:45:00.090
The whole thing, there are only
four rods in the center, all

00:45:00.090 --> 00:45:01.470
suspended.

00:45:01.470 --> 00:45:03.440
And the rest are all cables.

00:45:03.440 --> 00:45:07.500
It's cables and four
rods, and that's all.

00:45:07.500 --> 00:45:11.540
And it has one other
very exciting byproduct.

00:45:15.761 --> 00:45:17.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:17.170 --> 00:45:20.310
Clearly, I was very proud of it.

00:45:20.310 --> 00:45:22.470
Another very important
byproduct of this

00:45:22.470 --> 00:45:25.980
is the prismatic
effect of the glass.

00:45:25.980 --> 00:45:29.370
You see the glass,
because we don't

00:45:29.370 --> 00:45:32.100
have to keep water
or rain out of this.

00:45:32.100 --> 00:45:35.910
Because inside, we can
polish the edges of the glass

00:45:35.910 --> 00:45:37.170
and bevel it.

00:45:37.170 --> 00:45:40.830
By beveling it, the spectrum
of the colors came out.

00:45:40.830 --> 00:45:45.990
So at times you see,
a sunny day like this,

00:45:45.990 --> 00:45:47.950
it's just a rainbow inside.

00:45:47.950 --> 00:45:49.200
It's really quite spectacular.

00:45:49.200 --> 00:45:52.560
If you go there, make sure you
go there on a good, sunny day.

00:45:56.570 --> 00:45:59.640
Now, the Richelieu
wing, there was

00:45:59.640 --> 00:46:02.790
one thing has to be done
to the Richelieu wing.

00:46:02.790 --> 00:46:05.890
Because it's about the
painting collection

00:46:05.890 --> 00:46:09.120
is perhaps the most important
French painting collection

00:46:09.120 --> 00:46:11.340
in the world-- not
perhaps, definitely.

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:13.140
And yet, people don't go there.

00:46:13.140 --> 00:46:15.900
Because the French
conservators want it

00:46:15.900 --> 00:46:18.460
upstairs because of daylight.

00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:23.140
They are very, very
insistent on using daylight--

00:46:23.140 --> 00:46:25.350
our conservators are less so--

00:46:25.350 --> 00:46:26.850
very insistent.

00:46:26.850 --> 00:46:29.490
And for that reason, they
take the attic space.

00:46:29.490 --> 00:46:31.560
They have a ceiling
not very high.

00:46:31.560 --> 00:46:33.520
But to get up there,
you have to walk up

00:46:33.520 --> 00:46:36.360
75 feet, vertical space.

00:46:36.360 --> 00:46:38.760
And most people
don't walk up there.

00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:41.220
So they miss a lot of visitors.

00:46:41.220 --> 00:46:44.730
I was told only about, maybe at
the most, 10% or 15% of people

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:46.160
go up to the top floor.

00:46:46.160 --> 00:46:47.400
And that's a great pity.

00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:49.740
So I proposed to
put in escalators.

00:46:49.740 --> 00:46:51.060
I hate to do that.

00:46:51.060 --> 00:46:52.680
It's a 19th century
building, and you

00:46:52.680 --> 00:46:55.410
don't install
something like that

00:46:55.410 --> 00:46:57.060
unless you have a good reason.

00:46:57.060 --> 00:46:58.380
That was a big battle.

00:46:58.380 --> 00:46:59.860
But it was won.

00:46:59.860 --> 00:47:04.200
And today, nobody disagree
that it's absolutely needed.

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:07.980
Because otherwise,
the interconnection up

00:47:07.980 --> 00:47:12.038
and vertically, it's
very, very difficult.

00:47:12.038 --> 00:47:13.413
This is objet d'art.

00:47:13.413 --> 00:47:14.830
I show you the
sculpture actually.

00:47:14.830 --> 00:47:15.997
I show you the object d'art.

00:47:18.510 --> 00:47:22.680
These are the Maximilian
tapestries, never shown

00:47:22.680 --> 00:47:23.520
before--

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:24.720
no place to show it.

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:26.310
Now, they have a place.

00:47:26.310 --> 00:47:27.420
And it's a must.

00:47:27.420 --> 00:47:30.630
The Maximilian tapestries
are a must to see.

00:47:35.770 --> 00:47:38.680
They are very dimly lit because
of the color of the threads.

00:47:43.240 --> 00:47:46.830
Now, before I go
into this, this work

00:47:46.830 --> 00:47:52.440
here was done by architect
Willmotte of France--

00:47:52.440 --> 00:47:54.580
of Paris, France.

00:47:54.580 --> 00:47:57.990
And my role in the
Sculpture Garden and this

00:47:57.990 --> 00:48:00.860
is what they call,
really, a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:48:00.860 --> 00:48:02.730
It's sort of like a coordinator.

00:48:02.730 --> 00:48:05.680
I participated in all
the decision-making.

00:48:05.680 --> 00:48:08.910
I chose them as my architects.

00:48:08.910 --> 00:48:10.170
I had that responsibility.

00:48:10.170 --> 00:48:12.014
But they should get
the credit for it.

00:48:14.800 --> 00:48:16.620
Now, this was old Louvre.

00:48:19.730 --> 00:48:25.740
The lighting of paintings is a
really, a very special science.

00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:26.420
It really is.

00:48:26.420 --> 00:48:28.620
And it's extremely important.

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:30.327
It's never studied enough.

00:48:30.327 --> 00:48:31.160
It just hasn't been.

00:48:31.160 --> 00:48:34.700
We don't have good daylight
galleries in America.

00:48:34.700 --> 00:48:36.620
We don't have it in
the National Gallery.

00:48:36.620 --> 00:48:38.870
We don't have it in the
Museum of Fine Arts in Boston.

00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:40.340
They're all like this.

00:48:40.340 --> 00:48:45.500
They are laylights
with skylight on top,

00:48:45.500 --> 00:48:48.450
even though it could have
been artificially lit,

00:48:48.450 --> 00:48:50.470
and you wouldn't
know the difference.

00:48:50.470 --> 00:48:54.680
And another disadvantage is that
the skylight is very bright.

00:48:54.680 --> 00:48:57.800
And therefore, the
brightest is the ceiling.

00:48:57.800 --> 00:49:00.140
The second brightest
is the floor.

00:49:00.140 --> 00:49:04.640
The walls, where you want
the light to be, looks dark.

00:49:04.640 --> 00:49:07.910
And consequently,
we decided this

00:49:07.910 --> 00:49:11.190
is something we want to
do a piece of research on.

00:49:11.190 --> 00:49:14.660
And I think one of the
major breakthrough,

00:49:14.660 --> 00:49:17.270
I consider, in this
wing is in the lighting.

00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:26.370
The lighting solution is this.

00:49:26.370 --> 00:49:31.040
We make the ceiling
into three layers.

00:49:31.040 --> 00:49:34.990
The first layer's glass,
skylight, with a UV filter,

00:49:34.990 --> 00:49:36.550
of course.

00:49:36.550 --> 00:49:40.730
And then, below the
glass is an egg crate.

00:49:40.730 --> 00:49:45.910
And egg crate is
carefully calculated

00:49:45.910 --> 00:49:49.390
so that the orientation
is such so no direct sun

00:49:49.390 --> 00:49:51.790
rays will come in.

00:49:51.790 --> 00:49:54.190
It would have been better
if they were movable.

00:49:54.190 --> 00:49:58.210
Because then we get good light
all year round, all seasons.

00:49:58.210 --> 00:50:00.790
Unfortunately, the
French have experience

00:50:00.790 --> 00:50:03.730
with maintenance crew.

00:50:03.730 --> 00:50:05.460
They say it won't work here.

00:50:05.460 --> 00:50:09.460
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:09.460 --> 00:50:11.040
And they are right.

00:50:11.040 --> 00:50:12.580
They're right.

00:50:12.580 --> 00:50:15.910
So we use fixed louvers that
don't have to be touched.

00:50:15.910 --> 00:50:17.710
We cut off a lot of
light-- a lot more

00:50:17.710 --> 00:50:19.150
light than we wanted to.

00:50:19.150 --> 00:50:25.870
But it does remove the
headaches of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:25.870 --> 00:50:28.340
that was doing that job.

00:50:28.340 --> 00:50:30.160
And the reason of
the big cross is

00:50:30.160 --> 00:50:31.517
that people have to walk there.

00:50:31.517 --> 00:50:33.100
So it has to be wide
enough for people

00:50:33.100 --> 00:50:36.730
to walk, to clean, to relamp,
and that sort of thing.

00:50:36.730 --> 00:50:40.840
But you can see the sky if
you are walking on one side.

00:50:40.840 --> 00:50:45.790
But the light, as you see,
now is deflected to the walls.

00:50:45.790 --> 00:50:48.860
It's no longer coming
down to the floor.

00:50:48.860 --> 00:50:52.030
So the walls now are bright.

00:50:52.030 --> 00:50:53.730
And they get light.

00:50:53.730 --> 00:50:58.120
And that is turned
out to be something

00:50:58.120 --> 00:51:01.120
that-- the French conservatives
are very conservative,

00:51:01.120 --> 00:51:02.380
and they accepted this.

00:51:02.380 --> 00:51:05.810
And they now claim this
is the best in the world.

00:51:05.810 --> 00:51:08.120
They have to-- something
has to be, always.

00:51:08.120 --> 00:51:10.630
And this, they like.

00:51:10.630 --> 00:51:15.130
And this is a very
important suite

00:51:15.130 --> 00:51:20.350
of paintings by Rubens
celebrating Marie de' Medici's

00:51:20.350 --> 00:51:25.870
journey and eventually
to apotheosis.

00:51:25.870 --> 00:51:28.280
All the way, and
you can see again,

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:30.410
the light is no longer
bright on the ceiling.

00:51:30.410 --> 00:51:32.300
It's directed to the walls.

00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:35.110
And this is another
version of it.

00:51:35.110 --> 00:51:36.820
This is octagonal room.

00:51:36.820 --> 00:51:38.580
That's a long room.

00:51:38.580 --> 00:51:42.550
And the previous one, I
think, is a square room.

00:51:42.550 --> 00:51:44.410
And this is the way
the light looks--

00:51:44.410 --> 00:51:47.230
no reflection, no reflection.

00:51:47.230 --> 00:51:48.640
I guarantee you that--

00:51:48.640 --> 00:51:49.875
no reflection.

00:52:00.380 --> 00:52:01.540
All right.

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:05.530
Richelieu wing was
finished in November,

00:52:05.530 --> 00:52:12.200
open in November 1993, exactly
200 years after the founding

00:52:12.200 --> 00:52:13.400
of the Louvre.

00:52:13.400 --> 00:52:18.320
And the Richelieu wing, together
with the Napoleon Court,

00:52:18.320 --> 00:52:20.210
is now complete.

00:52:20.210 --> 00:52:23.500
And therefore, Louvre
finally functions

00:52:23.500 --> 00:52:26.960
as the way we had planned to.

00:52:26.960 --> 00:52:29.840
And some of these
slides are mostly

00:52:29.840 --> 00:52:35.240
to show you what it looks
like when it's all finished.

00:52:35.240 --> 00:52:40.700
And young lady is
celebrating the event.

00:52:40.700 --> 00:52:43.460
So there you are.

00:52:43.460 --> 00:52:47.080
I think that's-- oh.

00:52:47.080 --> 00:52:48.643
I've used up too much time.

00:52:48.643 --> 00:52:50.310
Well, sorry.

00:52:50.310 --> 00:52:52.110
[APPLAUSE]

00:52:56.010 --> 00:52:57.498
I'm sorry.

